steps.body:
A nightly job backtests a strategy on every symbol. Run them all in one process, and one bad symbol that crashes it takes every result with it. Run each one in a process of its own, and the supervisor that started them reports the crash and keeps the rest.

`fork()` from `<unistd.h>` makes a new process, the **child**, as a copy of the one that called it, the **parent**: the same code, a copy of every variable, the same open file descriptors, and the same place in the program, just after the call. So `fork` returns twice, once in each process:

- In the child it returns 0.
- In the parent it returns the child's process id, a `pid_t`.
- When the kernel can't make a process, it returns -1 in the parent, and there's no child.

The copy is cheap because no page of memory is copied at first, only the tables that map them. The two processes share every page, marked read-only, and the first write to one is a page fault, as in *Memory Hierarchy: Registers, Caches, RAM, Pages, and Disk*, in which the kernel copies just that page. That's called **copy-on-write**. Each process has its own virtual address space, so a change in one never shows in the other:

```cpp
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int fills = 10;
std::cout << std::flush;
const pid_t pid = fork();
if (pid == 0) {
    fills += 5;    // the child's own copy
    _exit(fills);  // the child ends here, with status 15
}
int status = 0;
waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
std::cout << "child's status " << WEXITSTATUS(status) << ", parent's fills " << fills << '\n';  // child's status 15, parent's fills 10
```

The child ends with `_exit`, not `return` or `std::exit`. It holds a copy of the parent's `std::cout` buffer, and a normal exit would flush that copy, printing the parent's unsent text twice. `_exit` ends the process at once, without flushing or running destructors. For the same reason, flush `std::cout` before every `fork`.

`waitpid(pid, &status, 0)` from `<sys/wait.h>` waits for that child to end and fills in `status`. `WIFEXITED(status)` says it exited, and `WEXITSTATUS(status)` gives its exit status. `WIFSIGNALED(status)` says a signal ended it, and `WTERMSIG(status)` gives the signal's number.

Until its parent waits, a child that has ended stays in the kernel's process table as a **zombie**, holding its id and its status. A supervisor that never waits fills the table with them. Here is a child on this Mac, seen by `ps` before and after its parent's `waitpid`:

```text
  PID  PPID STAT COMM
46159 46158 Z    <defunct>
  PID  PPID STAT COMM
```

When a parent ends first, process 1 adopts its children and waits for them: `systemd` or `init` on Linux, `launchd` on a Mac. Windows has no `fork`: `CreateProcess` starts a program, and `GetExitCodeProcess` reads how it ended.

example.code:
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// One backtest worker, the whole life of a child process: it ends in _exit or a crash.
void runBacktest(const std::string& symbol) {
    if (symbol == "TSLA") {
        std::abort();  // a bug in this worker: SIGABRT ends it
    }
    const int badRows = symbol == "MSFT" ? 3 : 0;  // stands in for checking the data
    _exit(badRows == 0 ? 0 : 1);
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> symbols = {"AAPL", "MSFT", "TSLA"};
    std::vector<pid_t> children;
    std::cout << "starting " << symbols.size() << " workers\n" << std::flush;  // nothing left to copy
    for (const std::string& symbol : symbols) {
        const pid_t pid = fork();
        if (pid < 0) {
            break;  // no more processes: wait for the ones already running
        }
        if (pid == 0) {
            runBacktest(symbol);  // the child, which never comes back here
        }
        children.push_back(pid);  // the parent
    }
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < children.size(); ++i) {
        int status = 0;
        waitpid(children[i], &status, 0);  // waits for this child, then reaps it
        std::cout << symbols[i] << ": ";
        if (WIFEXITED(status)) {
            std::cout << "exited with status " << WEXITSTATUS(status) << '\n';
        } else if (WIFSIGNALED(status)) {
            std::cout << "killed by signal " << WTERMSIG(status) << '\n';
        }
    }
    std::cout << "the supervisor is still running\n";
    return 0;
}

