steps.body:
A nightly report wants the day's fills in price order, and every system already has a program for that, `sort`. Running it from C++ takes three pieces: a pipe to carry its output back, a way to point its descriptors at the pipe, and a way to swap in a different program.

A **pipe**, the kernel buffer behind the shell's `|` in *Processes and System Calls*, has two file descriptors: `pipe(fds)` sets `fds[0]` to the read end and `fds[1]` to the write end. Bytes go through in order with no message boundaries, like a `SOCK_STREAM` socket in *Networking: Sockets, TCP, UDP, and Market Data Feeds*. An empty pipe makes `read` wait, and a full one, 64 KB on Linux and on this Mac, makes `write` wait.

`fork` copies descriptors, so a pipe made before it links parent and child, and each one closes the end it doesn't use. That isn't tidiness. `read` returns 0, the end of the data, only once every copy of the write end is closed, so a parent that keeps its own copy open waits forever. A process that writes to a pipe nobody reads gets `SIGPIPE`, which ends it, and that's how `yes | head -1` stops.

```cpp
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int fds[2];
pipe(fds);
std::cout << std::flush;
if (fork() == 0) {
    close(fds[0]);  // the child only writes
    write(fds[1], "MSFT 41022\n", 11);
    _exit(0);
}
close(fds[1]);  // the parent only reads, so now read can see the end
char buf[64];
std::string got;
ssize_t n = 0;
while ((n = read(fds[0], buf, sizeof buf)) > 0) {
    got.append(buf, n);
}
wait(nullptr);  // waits for any child
std::cout << "parent read " << got;  // parent read MSFT 41022
```

The **exec** functions replace the running program with another one, keeping the process, its id, and its open descriptors. `execvp` searches `PATH` like the shell, and returns only if it fails. Called right before it, `dup2(fds[1], 1)` makes descriptor 1 a copy of the write end, so the new program's standard output goes into the pipe. A shell runs `ls | sort` with one pipe, two forks, two `dup2` calls, and two execs.

`posix_spawn` and `posix_spawnp` from `<spawn.h>` do the fork and the exec in one call, with a list of **file actions**, like that `dup2`, for the child to run in between. Prefer them for starting programs: forking a big process copies its page tables, which `posix_spawn` can skip, and on a Mac it's a single system call. They return 0 or an error number, not -1. Windows has `CreatePipe` and `CreateProcess`.

